<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Narva (1700)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> was fought in 1700 at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, when about 8,000 Swedish troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> routed a much larger Russian army besieging Narva. It was one of the finest tactical victories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> the defeat prompted Peter I's military reforms, which created a standing professional army on the Western European model.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-narva-1700.jpg" alt="Battle of Narva (1700)" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustaf Cederström · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narva_(Gustaf_Cederstr%C3%B6m)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_18638.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> wanted an adequate opening to the Baltic and so sought the Swedish Baltic provinces that Russia had lost during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. Sweden, by contrast, had a professional army, one of the largest and most disciplined in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Russia, Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and Augustus the Strong of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> allied against Sweden, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> landed at Humlebæk north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and forced Denmark-Norway out of the alliance in August 1700. He then took part of his army across the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>.</p><p>The Russian army was poorly drilled. In spring 1700 Peter retired or reassigned most of his officers, leaving up to 70 percent of company posts vacant, and filled them with young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> noblemen with no command experience. In November Russian troops besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>. Peter left the camp the day before the battle, and the new commander, Charles Eugène de Croÿ, had never commanded a Russian unit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> on 29 November 1700 and the next morning positioned his army before the Russian siege lines. The Russians, stretched over four miles between two ramparts, outnumbered the relief force three or four to one. The Swedes moved forward at 2 pm, just as a snowstorm blew into the Russian faces. Charles ignored officers who wished to wait, and the Swedes attacked in two dense columns and, within 15 minutes, had filled the ditches with fascines and broken in.</p><p>The Swedes rolled up the Russian line to north and south, shattering inexperienced regiments one by one. Panic followed, and de Croÿ hurried to surrender. The Kamperholm Bridge, the only one across the Narva River, collapsed under retreating troops. Only the Preobrazhensky and Semyonovsky regiments held, in a square behind improvised barricades of wagons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Russian high command lost its morale and capitulated. The Swedes captured ten generals and ten colonels, 4,050 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and 173 artillery pieces. Regimental rolls of January 1701 show a loss of about 25 percent of personnel. Charles kept the senior Russian commanders as prisoners, explaining that he had not received the army's treasury.</p><p>The battle was not followed by a Swedish advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and Charles turned south against Augustus the Strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Peter took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in a second battle in 1704. A Russian memorial to the fallen was begun in 1900, and a Swedish one reading Svecia memor was inaugurated in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Narva (1700)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
